ne NIC (eth0), applied symmetric iptables DROP rules on node2 and node3 targeting each other's IPs, and verified bidirectional isolation while node1 remained reachable from both. Persistence was achieved via idempotent /etc/rc.local entries (no iptables-persistent package available) and directly validated by rebooting node2 and node3 and re-observing the isolation and node1-reachability post-reboot. The mandatory node-restart evidence protocol was completed for all three nodes with clear pre/post boot_id changes and recovered service state, cross-checked against the fixed lifecycle snapshots. Minor troubleshooting residue (a mistaken eth1 static address, reverted; a docker0 address deletion with unconfirmed but plausible self-correction via reboot) slightly lowers hygiene but does not affect task correctness.","limitations":["The docker0 address removed by cmd-42e5866b681048eb ('ip addr del 240.127.1.1/24 dev docker0') is not explicitly shown being restored in the trace; it is plausible this is regenerated automatically by the container runtime or was restored by node1's subsequent reboot, but this cannot be directly confirmed from the captured evidence.","Isolation was demonstrated only via ICMP ping; no evidence of testing with other protocols (TCP/UDP) was captured, though the iptables rules as written would block all IP traffic between the two peer addresses regardless of protocol."]},"ttft_ms":25486,"type":"result","duration_ms":79525,"uuid":"a891e7e7-e3b4-4c20-8f1e-606b1bd048c2","ttft_stream_ms":1258,"time_to_request_ms":37,"first_content_frame_ms":1825,"queued_turn_count":0}
